1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,860
Venus is the planet most like Earth.

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:04,740
So what happened to it?

3
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:09,160
This is the ground on Venus, seen
through the clouds by radar.

4
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:10,760
Why radar?

5
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:13,200
Because ordinary light cannot get through.

6
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:15,520
It is hot enough to melt lead.

7
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:19,400
The air presses down like the
sea nearly a kilometre deep.

8
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:23,920
No machine we have landed there could
survive it for much more than two hours.

9
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:27,420
And this world is almost
the size of yours.

10
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:29,400
It is made of the same rock.

11
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:32,360
It even takes in less
sunlight than Earth does,

12
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,640
and it is still the hottest
planet in the solar system.

13
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:38,980
In this film I will show
you how that can be true,

14
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:43,240
where its water went, and
why the answer is still open.

15
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:46,380
Start with what the two planets share.

16
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:53,360
Venus is ninety-five percent as wide as
Earth, with about four-fifths of its mass.

17
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:56,220
It formed from the same kind of rock,

18
00:00:56,220 --> 00:01:00,520
in the same young solar system,
at nearly the same time.

19
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:06,100
From a distance, it is the closest
thing to a second Earth that we know.

20
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:09,440
It is also closer to the Sun.

21
00:01:09,980 --> 00:01:13,940
Venus receives almost twice
the sunlight that reaches us.

22
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:19,240
So the easy answer is that it
is simply nearer the fire.

23
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:23,240
But that answer breaks on one number.

24
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:28,140
Venus is wrapped in bright
cloud that reflects about

25
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:32,000
three-quarters of that sunlight
straight back to space.

26
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:36,560
So Venus actually soaks up
less sunlight than Earth does.

27
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,500
Less light in, and still a surface at
four hundred and sixty-five degrees.

28
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:44,960
It sounds like a paradox.

29
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:48,620
It stops being one when
you look at the air.

30
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:53,100
So the heat is not about
how much light arrives.

31
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:56,460
It is about what happens to
the heat once it is there.

32
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:59,040
It cannot get out.

33
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:01,980
What would you see if you stood there?

34
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:03,960
Not darkness.

35
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:10,180
The clouds let through a dim, orange daylight,
like a heavy evening that never ends.

36
00:02:10,699 --> 00:02:13,020
You would never see the Sun itself.

37
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:17,620
The wind at the ground moves at
little more than a slow walk,

38
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:22,600
but in air that thick, even a
slow wind pushes like a river.

39
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:26,880
The air of Venus is ninety-six
percent carbon dioxide,

40
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:28,620
and there is a great deal of it.

41
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:34,000
Carbon dioxide lets sunlight through
but blocks the heat trying to leave.

42
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:40,340
On Earth it is a thin trace in the air,
and even that trace keeps the planet warm.

43
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:46,600
On Venus the whole sky is made of
it, ninety times thicker than ours.

44
00:02:46,840 --> 00:02:48,360
The heat goes in.

45
00:02:48,380 --> 00:02:50,140
It does not come out.

46
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:56,760
Day and night, pole and equator, the ground
sits at nearly the same temperature.

47
00:02:57,560 --> 00:02:58,960
Here is the turn.

48
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:02,700
Earth has roughly as much
carbon dioxide as Venus.

49
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:04,600
It is just not in the air.

50
00:03:04,640 --> 00:03:06,040
It is in rock.

51
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:11,040
Limestone, chalk and seabed,
built over billions of years.

52
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:14,120
Rain and rivers carry it to the sea,

53
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:17,500
and sea life and chemistry
lock it into the floor.

54
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:22,000
The thing that pulled Earth's
carbon out of the sky was water.

55
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:24,160
So the question changes.

56
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:27,800
It is not why Venus has
so much carbon dioxide.

57
00:03:27,980 --> 00:03:32,020
It is why Venus has no
water to put it away.

58
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:34,680
We make one of these every week.

59
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:37,900
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

60
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:40,420
The water is the mystery.

61
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:44,140
And Venus left a clue in
the air about where it went.

62
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:48,140
In 1978 a NASA probe
fell through the clouds

63
00:03:48,140 --> 00:03:51,580
of Venus and tasted the
air on the way down.

64
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:56,080
It found a trace of water,
and something odd inside it.

65
00:03:56,660 --> 00:03:59,140
Some hydrogen is heavier than the rest.

66
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:01,060
It is called deuterium.

67
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:04,420
On Venus, the share of heavy hydrogen is

68
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:07,140
around a hundred times
higher than on Earth.

69
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:09,500
That number is a fingerprint.

70
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:13,540
High in the air, sunlight
breaks water apart.

71
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:18,060
The light hydrogen escapes to space
more easily than the heavy kind.

72
00:04:18,579 --> 00:04:22,359
Lose enough water that way,
over a long enough time,

73
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:25,100
and the heavy hydrogen is what remains.

74
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:28,440
Venus has lost a great deal of water.

75
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:32,400
How much, and in what
form, is still unknown.

76
00:04:32,820 --> 00:04:34,520
Was it ever an ocean?

77
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:37,140
Spin matters here.

78
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:41,780
Venus turns once every two hundred
and forty-three Earth days,

79
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:43,680
and it turns backwards.

80
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:47,460
A single day there is
longer than its whole year.

81
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:51,180
That slow turn decides
where the clouds gather.

82
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:53,980
One model says it may have been.

83
00:04:54,400 --> 00:05:00,260
In 2016 a team at NASA's Goddard
Institute ran early Venus with that

84
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:05,320
slow spin and found that thick
daytime clouds could shade the ground.

85
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:08,440
In their model, Venus may have had shallow

86
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:12,300
oceans and mild weather
for billions of years.

87
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:16,060
Another model says the
water never came down.

88
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:21,420
In 2021 a team led by Martin
Turbet found that clouds

89
00:05:21,420 --> 00:05:25,760
gathered on the night side
instead, trapping heat like a lid.

90
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:30,880
The steam stayed in the sky and
the oceans may never have formed.

91
00:05:31,340 --> 00:05:34,540
Both are careful models,
and they disagree.

92
00:05:35,060 --> 00:05:38,340
Nobody has yet read the
rocks that could settle it.

93
00:05:38,980 --> 00:05:42,440
Either way, Venus ended in the same place.

94
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:44,100
Water gone.

95
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:45,680
Carbon in the sky.

96
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:47,900
Heat with nowhere to go.

97
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:50,520
So why Venus and not Earth?

98
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:52,580
Distance is part of it.

99
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:55,760
Nearer the Sun, there is more
light to start the climb.

100
00:05:55,940 --> 00:06:01,780
Warmer air holds more water vapour, and
water vapour is itself a greenhouse gas.

101
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:06,840
Past a certain line, the steam reaches
high enough for sunlight to split it,

102
00:06:06,860 --> 00:06:09,860
and the water starts
leaking away to space.

103
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:13,300
Venus sits on the wrong side of that line.

104
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:16,740
Earth sits on the safe side, for now.

105
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:20,260
Last week we saw the Sun getting brighter,

106
00:06:20,260 --> 00:06:23,820
about one percent every
hundred and ten million years.

107
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:26,520
That climb moves the line outward.

108
00:06:26,740 --> 00:06:29,520
In about a billion
years, it reaches Earth.

109
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:32,340
This is not a warning about next century.

110
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:36,880
The warming measured on Earth today is
real, and it is a different ledger.

111
00:06:37,420 --> 00:06:40,420
Studies of Earth's climate
find that burning our fossil

112
00:06:40,420 --> 00:06:44,660
fuels is very unlikely to
start a full runaway like this.

113
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:49,260
Venus is a story told in
hundreds of millions of years.

114
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:52,700
What it shows is how
thin the difference is.

115
00:06:52,860 --> 00:06:57,260
Same rock, same start, and
one turn with the water.

116
00:06:57,660 --> 00:07:00,080
The answer is waiting on the ground.

117
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:05,540
Venus has repainted most of its surface
with lava in the last billion years or so.

118
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:09,680
But some high, crumpled regions
called tesserae look older.

119
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:14,780
If water ever shaped Venus, rocks
like those may still remember it.

120
00:07:15,480 --> 00:07:16,960
People have landed there.

121
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:21,460
In 1982 a Soviet lander
photographed the ground

122
00:07:21,460 --> 00:07:24,360
and lasted about two
hours before the heat won.

123
00:07:24,940 --> 00:07:29,800
In 2021 a probe on its way to the
Sun looked at the night side of

124
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:34,100
Venus and saw the faint glow of
the hot surface through the clouds.

125
00:07:34,820 --> 00:07:37,500
The next step is to read
the rocks properly.

126
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:41,980
NASA chose two missions
to go back: DAVINCI,

127
00:07:41,980 --> 00:07:46,600
designed to drop through the air and
photograph the old highlands of Alpha Regio,

128
00:07:46,720 --> 00:07:51,660
and VERITAS, designed to map the
surface and its rock types from orbit.

129
00:07:52,240 --> 00:07:54,980
Europe has its own orbiter, EnVision.

130
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:58,320
The planet next door
is the clearest record

131
00:07:58,320 --> 00:08:01,580
we have of how a world like ours can turn.

132
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:03,660
Not by falling apart.

133
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:07,200
By keeping its heat, and losing its water.

134
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:10,760
What if Venus was never the monster,

135
00:08:10,819 --> 00:08:14,360
but a twin that simply
ran the experiment first?

136
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:17,860
The bigger question hangs in your own sky,

137
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:21,180
the brightest thing at dawn
or dusk after the Moon.

138
00:08:21,780 --> 00:08:24,880
How close did Earth come to the same turn?

139
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:29,500
Next week: what happens to your
clock near the speed of light.
